  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The velocity vector $v=\u27e8u,v\u27e9,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=\u27e83{t}^{2}+2t-1,-4t+5\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the acceleration is\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+5)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86t+2,\\,-4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =\u27e86(1)+2,-4\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e88,-4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|\u27e88,-4\u27e9| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is $4\\sqrt{5}{\\text{\u00a0m/s}}^{2}.$",
